A||||6||KevinP||Erratic Idle||||1||
Z||000000||KevinP||08-14-2000||12:07 AM||||Since I first got my 84 SE, whenever I start it up, it idles at around 2500 rpm's, and doesn't go down for 5-10 minutes, or until the car warms up.  Sometimes it will go down to 1000, then swing right back up past 2000.  In any case, until it gets good and warm, it idles wherever it pleases.  could it be the throttle cable?  I already gave it a good look and it doesn't seem to be sticking at all.  Anyone ever see this before?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||ur2biteme||08-14-2000||03:59 AM||||well, i have an '87 with the DIS motor, and it was and currently is doing something similar.  MUCH less dramatic now though.  it lopes a little when cold until i give it a little gas then it idles at about 1500 until it warms up, then it idles between 800-1000, somewhat roughly.  <P>i was going through the same problems and it was a plugged muffler.  the car was wrecked by the previous owner in the passenger rear q.p. and nothing was repaired.  however, it did foul some sensors.<P>first check your computer codes.  more than likely it has set a code for the idle air control valve.  possibly the o2 sensor.  then check your exhaust system for leaks, or a crumpled (or in my case BULGING, it blew up internaly)muffler.  <P>if that doesn't help, post more info and i'll try to see what i can remember of my fiasco.  btw, i attribute my current rough idle to 166k miles for sure, and possibly my bad cat. converter pluging things up, or my flowmaster opening things up more, or some strange combination of both.  <P>sorry for my rambling.  i hope this helps||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||08-14-2000||03:18 PM||||Both of you,<P>check for vacuum leaks. espcially around the base of the TBI. (If that 87 is also automatic, go read the article about the torque strut problem in the cave. link at bottom of this page)<P>Also check the coolant sensor. The old type used in 84 is prone to problems.<P>Crappy Throttle Position sensors can also cause temperature dependant idle problems.<P>UR2, take out the MAT sensor and see it it's burnt. If it is replace it. Get one from BorgWarner. the MAT is to the right of the TBI sticking straight up from the intake manifold. (Only 87-88 4's have MAT)||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fformula88||08-14-2000||05:18 PM||||Take a look at your EGR valve and make sure its down tight.  For some reason, the one on my dads 87 coupe had worked itself loose.  Both bolts were almost completely out and the darn valve was rattling all over.  Plus the gasket had broken and was partially plugging the hole.  After tightening it down it idled fine.<p>[This message has been edited by Fformula88 (edited 08-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Seanh||08-14-2000||09:11 PM||||first check the EGR valve. Then if thats not the problem, underneath the EGR valve, there is a pipe that goes somewhere back into the engine. I cant really be any more specific becuase Im not really into working on cars yet, But last year some water got into mine, it frozed and cracked, I purchased a flexable replacment from the Midwest Fiero owners club, and installed in, and it works good now. It is just a small metal pipe that goes into the engine somewhere and is covered with heat insulation. Im sure somone on here could tell you the exact name and more about what Im talking about.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Currently upgrading to a NorthStar.||||reg||1
Z||000005||ur2biteme||08-15-2000||06:30 AM||||well, i was trying to help kevinp, but thanks ogre  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  anyway, when i was fixing the problems, i replaced the coil packs, plugs, plug wires, crankshaft position sensor, throttle position sensor, oxygen sensor, muffler and the egr valve was loose and i tightened that down, this fixed about 75% of the problem.  the one things i didn't replace were the mat and the egr.  i'll take care of that when i get the more manditory stuff taken care of......drive axle.......inner tie rods....   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>btw, mine's a 5 speed if it helps.<P><P>------------------<BR>UR2BITEME<BR>'87 econo coupe<BR>165k and a bad fender :(||||reg||11
Z||000006||blade56_2001||08-15-2000||10:35 AM||||  I just forked over $400.00 bucks today for the same problem you all are have having accept to add to it, i'm only getting about 100 miles to the tank of gas?  I had the O-2 sensor replaced aswell as the idle control sensor and it still idles at 2 grand in park and 1200 in gear and the service engine light still pops up when im sitting at the stop light, the mech hooked it back up to the snap-on computer and came up code 35 something about the oxygen sensor messed up... he is taking it back in tomorrow. ain't that a pain in the butt, by the way it's an '86 se v-6 can't figure out why it's guzzling gas though?<BR>||||reg||8
